\paragraph{Prior decision styles as degenerate points on the frontier.}
The frontier also formalizes the positioning table: existing TTA decision styles are
special --- and weaker --- points of the same object.

\begin{proposition}[Always-adapt is the $\varepsilon\equiv0$ certificate]\label{prop:s1}
Always-adapt is the rule of Theorem~\ref{thm:cert} with $\varepsilon\equiv0$ and a
positive prior estimate (it is $\kappa$-blind). Its regret equals the full false-adapt
mass $\E[|\Delta|\mathbf1\{\Delta<0\}]$ (Proposition~\ref{thm:gate}), and on a
$(\delta,0)$-ambiguous pair its worst-case committal regret is $\delta$ --- twice the
Le Cam floor $\delta/2$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:gate-minimax} --- while the certificate
abstains (zero committal regret).
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
With $\varepsilon\equiv0$ every instance is committed and all harmful mass is adapted
into; the regret expression is the false-adapt term of the decomposition. On the
ambiguous pair the rule commits \textsc{adapt} in both worlds and is fully wrong in the
$\Delta<0$ world, paying $\delta=2\cdot(\delta/2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Confidence filters lack false-adapt control]\label{prop:s2}
Let $g$ commit \textsc{adapt} whenever a label-free confidence statistic exceeds a
threshold (the decision style of entropy filtering). There exist confidently-wrong worlds
--- harmful and helpful instances with identical confidence laws --- in which
$\mathrm{FA}(g)$ equals the harmful base rate, while the certificate keeps
$\mathrm{FA}\le\alpha$ on the same stream. The certificate's protection is purchased
precisely by the calibrated benefit sample behind $\varepsilon_\alpha$, not by any
heuristic on $Z$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Construct the pair as in Theorem~\ref{thm:imp} with the confidence statistic a function
of $X$ alone, concentrated above the threshold in both worlds. The filter's action law is
then identical across worlds and commits \textsc{adapt} with probability $\approx1$, so in
the harmful world $\mathrm{FA}$ equals the harmful mass. The certificate's bound is
world-independent (Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}).
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The decision is ordinal: it survives symmetric label noise]\label{prop:s3}
On the disagreement region $D$, let labels be corrupted by unknown symmetric noise
$\eta<\tfrac12$. Observed accuracies obey
$a'-\tfrac12=(1-2\eta)(a-\tfrac12)$, hence
$\sign(a_a'-a_0')=\sign(a_a-a_0)$: the adapt/freeze decision of
Theorem~\ref{thm:disagree} and Proposition~\ref{thm:disagree-mc} is invariant to $\eta$, while the
absolute risks $R_T(f_0),R_T(f_a)$ are confounded by $\eta$ and not identifiable.
Label-free \emph{accuracy estimation} (the AETTA/ATC target) therefore solves a strictly
harder problem than the decision requires.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$a'=(1-\eta)a+\eta(1-a)=a(1-2\eta)+\eta$, which is the stated affine map; it is
sign-preserving around $\tfrac12$ for $\eta<\tfrac12$ and not invertible without $\eta$.
\end{proof}

\noindent\emph{Numerically validated.} S1: on a mixed synthetic stream the always-adapt
regret is $455.3$ versus $0.0$ committal regret for the certificate; on the witness pair,
$1.0$ versus the $0.5$ floor. S2: in a confidently-wrong world the filter's false-adapt
rate is $0.501$ (the harmful share) versus $0.000\le\alpha{=}0.1$ for the certificate.
S3: the decision sign is preserved at every $\eta\in\{0,\dots,0.4\}$ while the cardinal
accuracy bias grows to $0.096$ at $\eta{=}0.4$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:subsume}).

